d be scattering of  the laser light right pretty simple and  what the detector does is to collect the  scattering light the scattering light  which are like a low angle are collected  by this forward scatter detector and  these light scatter which is collected  by the forward scatter detector and any  other detector can tell us a lot about  its complexity and size and we would  know how other than like forward scatter  light could be scattering in any other  direction all the directions like any  light scatter which is of a higher angle  can be diverted into another side  scattered detector and that could give  us another readout an important  parameter now let's talk about the  forward scatter first in its  characteristics  so imagine to situation in situation one  there is a big sail and in a situation  to there is a small sail so forward  scatter is kind of proportional to the  size of the sample or the cell if the  cell is big forward scatter would be the  magnitude of forward scatter is big and  if the cell is small the forward scatter  would be also small but apart from  forward scatter light can be also bent  into higher angles which would be  detected by side scatter detectors now  light would be scattered in higher  angles when the cellular complexity is  more when the light would really get a  lot of obstruction in while passing  through the sample right so a complex  sample would scatter light in more  different direction so the side scatter  would be more for a very complex sample  right so side scatter is kind of  proportional to complexity or  granularity of the  now all this fluidic system which  focuses the sale and the detection  system the PMT's would give you a  readout of cell count and the forward  scatter just like you can see the graphs  here but individually the forward  scatter data or the side scatter data  doesn't mean that much but we can  combine these two side scatter and the  forward scatter data to form like  special scatter plots where we can  identify special populations and  characteristics about that okay this  might not be very clear at this moment  let me give you an example so here you  can see all the three colors  corresponding to three different  populations but let us take a real-life  example so let's say we want to  differentiate between all the cell  populations present in our blood it  would be RBC is there would be WBC's  even in the WBC's there would be like  several type of WBC's having some  granular side some a granulocytes etc  right so imagine these three populations  the population at the top could  correspond to a very complex and  granular structure so look at these blue  population you can see the side scatter  is quite a lot as well as the forward  scatter is not so you can derive to  conclusion that these cell population is  pretty big because it has high sites the  forward scatter and also it's pretty  complex because it has high side scatter  as well compared to this green  population which has low forward scatter  and low side scatter as well you can see  based on the forward scatter and the  light scattering information we can  really group the cell into different sub  categories and not only grouping and  getting information about them and about  their morphology we can also sort them  out differently for further usage right  that's the best thing about flow  cytometry now in my other videos I'll be  talking about fluorescence assisted cell  sorting systems and for now that's it so  if you like my video to be liquid comes  up and don't forget to like share  subscribe thank you
 

